# The composed layers whose own totals cannot be subtracted. One row per composed layer carrying at
# least one part whose conversion factor is a range, or has no figure to say whether it is.
#
# A derived total settles like a filed one
#   A composed layer that files its demand or nameplate as its fusion's sum is read at the figure
#   its fusion computes (layers/demand.sqlc, layers/nameplate.sqlc), and where its conversions are
#   single values that figure can be subtracted as legitimately as a filed one: the eliminations are
#   inside it, subtracted bound by bound, so its nameplate less its demand is the remainder worked
#   out from its parts. So it is not here, and a remainder walk stops on it. Where its figure cannot
#   be computed, the walk stops on a layer with no figure, which
#   composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc suspends and algebra/settled_remainders.sqlc counts.
#
# It reads the resolved parts, not the references, and that keeps two grounds apart
#   `composition/part_references.sqlc` would carry a part whose notation names no filing here, and
#   such a fusion is already suspended by `composition/unresolved_parts.sqlc`. Reading references
#   here would suspend it twice, on two grounds, one of which is not about conversions at all.
#   Composing `composition/parts.sqlc` is what keeps this ground and that one apart.
#
# A factor with no figure is not a single value
#   A conversion filed `unmeasured`, or as an identity's output nothing here computes, has no width
#   anybody can read, and comparing its two empty bounds would read it as a single value: a blank
#   taken for the value that makes subtracting the totals legitimate. Nobody knows whether the
#   factor scaled both totals with a range, so both states are unsettled by name, and only a stated
#   factor is asked for its width.
#
# Where the remainder's walk stops
#   `composition/leaves.sqlc` is where the parts bottom out, and it is where a sum over parts stops.
#   A remainder stops earlier: wherever subtracting the totals is legitimate, which is wherever the
#   two were not scaled by one factor that is a range. A layer at the bottom qualifies trivially,
#   having nothing above it that could tie the two together, but so does every composed layer whose
#   conversions are single values, and most of the corpus is those.
#
# A remainder walk stops at the first settled layer
#   So a walk for a remainder must stop at the first settled layer and not run to the bottom.
#   Running past one throws away a filed figure its composer stands behind, and with it every
#   correction that composer already applied, which then has to be rebuilt from below and can fail
#   for reasons the filed figure had already settled.
-- composition/parts.sqlc restricted to the parts whose factor has width or no figure.
SELECT DISTINCT p.composition AS filing, p.composed_layer AS layer
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
WHERE p.factor_state IN ('absent', 'derivation')
   OR (p.factor_state = 'stated' AND p.factor_low <> p.factor_high)
